## Why is professional services SEO different?

Professional services SEO is different because Google holds advice content to a higher standard. Legal, financial and health topics fall under what Google calls YMYL (Your Money or Your Life). Wrong information on these topics can cause real harm.

Google's quality guidelines ask raters to look for stronger evidence of experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trust (E-E-A-T) on these pages. The guidelines are not a ranking formula. They do show what Google wants its systems to reward.

Generic tactics fit these markets poorly. Keyword-stuffed service pages, templated location pages and thin blog posts give a reader no reason to trust the firm. Work that suits this market rests on four things:

1. **Topical depth:** expert-authored content that answers the questions clients ask before they hire.
2. **Entity signals:** clear facts about the firm and its practitioners that Google can cross-check.
3. **Technical health:** a site that search engines can crawl, render and index without friction.
4. **Local signals:** visibility for the way professional services buyers search near them.

## Why do professional services firms struggle with SEO?

Professional services firms usually struggle with SEO for one of four reasons.

**Generic SEO work that ignores regulated content.** A solicitor's site needs different evidence from a software company's site. Content that is technically adequate can still fail to show the expertise a legal, financial or advisory query needs.

**Thin service pages.** "We provide commercial litigation services" says very little. A competitor page that answers questions about process, costs, timelines and outcomes shows far more expertise. Depth signals a specialist. Breadth without depth looks like a directory.

**Authority split across many profiles.** A partner may have a LinkedIn profile, a legal directory listing, a firm bio and a personal website. Each one holds part of the picture. Consistent facts across all of them help Google connect the person to the firm.

**Technical debt on older websites.** Older firm websites often carry crawl problems, duplicate practice area pages, slow load times and missing structured data. These problems hold pages back whatever the content quality.

## AI visibility for professional services firms and B2B consultancies

Buyers of professional services now ask AI assistants for shortlists. A finance director might ask ChatGPT, Copilot or Google's AI Mode for "the best employment law firms for a UK tech company" or "AEO consultants for B2B consultancies in the UK". The assistant names a few firms and cites the sources it drew on. Firms missing from those answers may never reach the comparison stage.

AI assistants build answers from what they can find and cross-check about a firm. The same evidence that supports E-E-A-T in Google also makes a firm easier to cite.

| What AI assistants look for | What it means for your firm |
| --- | --- |
| Named experts with real credentials | Bio pages for partners and consultants with qualifications, regulator or membership details, specialisms and published work |
| Specific service pages | One page per service or practice area that says who it is for, how it works and what it costs or how fees are set |
| Third-party mentions | Accurate listings in the directories, rankings, professional bodies and press your buyers already trust |
| Consistent facts | The same firm name, people, locations, services and founding details on your site, profiles and directories |
| Clear answers to buyer questions | Plain answers to the questions buyers ask before they shortlist, written or reviewed by the expert who owns the topic |

### How I measure AI visibility

I measure it with a fixed prompt panel. The panel runs the same buyer questions on the same assistants each month and records the date and conditions. That makes month-on-month changes comparable. I report where your firm is named, where competitors are named instead and which sources the assistants cite.

AI answers vary from one run to the next and between users. I report what I observe. I do not guarantee that any assistant will mention or cite your firm.

### Where to start

The AI visibility audit is a £1,500 fixed-fee project delivered in 2 weeks. It sets your baseline across the main assistants, maps the sources they cite in your market and gives you a prioritised plan. Implementation is scoped separately.

